window.docContents[243].resolve({"/Environment/#Cvc___lean----High-Level-CVC5-API--Environment--___":{"contents":"This API abstracts away the term manager completely and enforces manager-consistency statically\nusing a type-class called Ω. Virtually all types in this library have an implicit Ω argument\nthat can be thought of as representing a specific term manager. On the sort type for instance:\n\n#check Srt\n\n\nCvc.Srt [Ω] : Type\n\n\nΩ is an empty Prop type-class and will be erased at runtime; note that you cannot build an\ninstance yourself as the constructor is private.\n\n\n\nΩ is tied to cvc.lean's environment monad Env: Env α, or more precisely @Env Ω α,\ncorresponds to code that uses a specific term manager to produce a value of type α.\n\nThe first problem Ω and Env solve is that manipulating a value v tied to manager m after\nm has been freed is unsafe as freeing m frees v, so v should only live as long as m is\nalive. This is why most features in this library produce an Env _ output.\n\nSecond, as mentioned above, values tied to different managers should never interact as that would\ncause a runtime error. By encoding a specific term manager through an Ω-value, we can type-check\nthat sorts/terms/solvers/etc. are compatible by making sure they agree on their Ω-value.\n\nFor example, the function building an array-sort makes sure the index and element sorts agree on\ntheir manager, and keeps track of the manager associated to the resulting sort by having all of\nthem agree on their Ω.\n\n\n\n","context":"Cvc.lean -- High-Level CVC5 API\u0009Environment","header":"1.1. Ω","id":"/Environment/#Cvc___lean----High-Level-CVC5-API--Environment--___"}});